        "titulo": "The present of -ir verbs",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: verbs in this group end in «-ir»: «vivir», «escribir», «abrir». The stem is what's left without the «-ir»: «viv-», «escrib-», «abr-». STEP 2: the present-tense endings are: -o, -es, -e, -imos, -ís, -en. «Vivo, vives, vive, vivimos, vivís, viven.» STEP 3: notice these are the same as the -er group except for two: «nosotros» is «-imos» (not «-emos») and «vosotros» is «-ís» (not «-éis»). The other four forms are identical. STEP 4: the accent mark on «vivís», «escribís» and «abrís» marks the stressed syllable: vi-VÍS. It's never written «viivís» with two «i»s — the accent is the only mark needed. STEP 5: since -er and -ir verbs are so close, learn all three groups side by side: in «yo», the ending is «-o» in all three; in «tú», it's «-as» or «-es»; in «él», it's «-a» or «-e». STEP 6: «vivir» also means to live/reside somewhere: «Vivo en Madrid» means «I live in Madrid». STEP 7: important false friend: «asistir» does NOT mean «to watch/attend as a spectator» the way it might look — it means to attend, to be present. «Asisto a clase» means «I go to class / I attend class». To watch a movie, say «ver una película». STEP 8: «recibir» means to receive; to say someone receives guests at home, use «recibir a» with the personal «a»: «Recibo a mis amigos». STEP 9: «abrir» and «subir» are regular in the present; their irregular past participles («abierto», «escrito») come up in lesson 17.",
        "comparacao": "English «to live» and «to write» map directly onto «vivir» and «escribir» in meaning, and the conjugation pattern itself needs to be learned as a set of six new endings rather than mapped from English grammar. The one real trap: «asistir» looks like it should mean «to assist» or «to watch», but it means «to attend, to be present» — for watching something, Spanish uses «ver».",
